<h2>The source of the power: recognised agents under Order III CPC</h2>
<p>The Code of Civil Procedure permits appearances, applications and acts in or before any court to be made or done by a party in person, by a recognised agent, or by a pleader. Recognised agents include persons holding powers of attorney authorising them to make such appearances, applications and acts on behalf of the party. Family Court proceedings follow this procedural framework, so a properly drawn POA gives its holder genuine procedural standing: this is not a grey area, and courts deal with attorney-filed matrimonial petitions from NRI parties every day.</p>
<p>The instrument must actually say what it is relied upon for. A general power "to manage my affairs" invites objection; a special power naming the court, the nature of the proceedings and the acts authorised — to file, sign and verify pleadings and affidavits, engage and instruct counsel, appear at hearings, make and receive payments, and take procedural steps — does not.</p>

<h2>What the attorney can do, and where the line falls</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h4>Within the attorney's power</h4>
    <p>File the petition or written statement; sign and verify pleadings; swear affidavits on matters of record and on the attorney's own knowledge; engage and instruct counsel; attend formal and procedural hearings; receive and make payments; take steps in execution and in interlocutory applications.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h4>Beyond the attorney's power</h4>
    <p>Depose to facts within the party's personal knowledge — the cruelty alleged, the desertion, the breakdown of the relationship, the party's state of mind; supply the party's consent in a mutual consent divorce; undergo the court's direct satisfaction of free will; make admissions on personal facts that bind the party as if the party had spoken.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The evidentiary limit deserves emphasis because it is the one most often misunderstood. An attorney may validly prove documents, narrate events the attorney personally witnessed, and speak to acts done as attorney. But matrimonial causes turn on personal facts — what passed between the spouses — and on those facts the deposition of an attorney is no evidence of the party's case at all. Courts have consistently declined to attach weight to an attorney's testimony on matters that could only be within the party's own knowledge. A petitioner abroad who expects a parent holding a POA to prove cruelty in the witness box is preparing a petition for dismissal; the realistic alternatives are the party's own deposition, in person or by video conferencing where the court permits it.</p>

<h2>Mutual consent divorce under Section 13B HMA: the strictest zone</h2>
<p>Section 13B of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955 requires a joint petition on the ground that the parties have been living separately, have not been able to live together, and have mutually agreed to dissolve the marriage; and at the second motion the court grants the decree only after hearing the parties and being satisfied, after such inquiry as it thinks fit, that the averments are true and the consent subsists. That satisfaction is personal to the parties. Consent is not a procedural step an agent performs — it is the jurisdictional fact itself.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h4>First motion</h4><p>Joint petition presented; the court records the parties' statements. Personal presence, or appearance by video conferencing where the court allows it, is the norm for the statements; a POA holder may, with leave, handle the formal presentation.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Interregnum</h4><p>The statutory waiting period runs; either party remains free to withdraw consent, which is precisely why consent cannot be delegated to an attorney in advance.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h4>Second motion</h4><p>The court satisfies itself directly that consent continues and is free. Here the insistence on hearing the party — physically or over video conferencing — is at its strongest; a POA appearance cannot substitute for the party's own statement.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>The practical consequence for NRI parties: plan the two motions around the party's availability, in person or by video conferencing with the court's permission, and use the POA holder for everything else — filing, coordination with counsel, compliance and collection of certified copies. The detailed video-conferencing practice for NRI mutual consent matters is covered in a separate article on this site.</p></div>

<h2>Exemption from personal appearance</h2>
<p>Outside the consent statements, matrimonial courts routinely require parties to appear in person at various stages — reconciliation efforts, mediation, recording of evidence. A party genuinely unable to travel may move an application for exemption from personal appearance, supported by concrete material: employment constraints, visa or immigration limitations, medical grounds, or the sheer distance and cost involved. Courts weigh such applications sensibly, often granting exemption for routine dates while reserving the stages where personal interaction matters — mediation and the recording of the party's own evidence — for appearance in person or by video conferencing. The POA holder's attendance on exempted dates keeps the proceedings moving and demonstrates bona fides.</p>

<h2>POAs executed abroad: getting the instrument right</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Execution before the competent authority:</strong> the instrument should be signed before a notary or equivalent authority in the country of residence and, where that country's practice requires, attested or legalised through consular or comparable channels, so that its due execution can be presumed or readily proved in India.</p>
  <p><strong>Specific, not general:</strong> name the proceedings, the court, the parties and the acts authorised. Add express authority for anything unusual the case will need — compromise, receipt of settlement amounts, execution steps.</p>
  <p><strong>Stamping on arrival:</strong> an instrument executed outside India attracts stamp duty when it is received and acted upon here, and it must be presented for adjudication and stamped within the time the stamp law prescribes. This is described here generally because the mechanics vary; what does not vary is that an unstamped foreign POA is an objection waiting to be taken.</p>
  <p><strong>Identity of the attorney:</strong> choose an attorney who can actually attend — a Delhi-resident parent or sibling — and keep their identity documents and the original instrument ready for the court's inspection.</p>
</div>
<p>Used within its limits, a power of attorney makes litigation from abroad workable: the attorney carries the procedure, the party supplies the testimony and the consent. Used beyond its limits, it produces petitions that stall at evidence or founder at the second motion. The distinction is not a technicality; it reflects the law's insistence that a marriage is dissolved, and matrimonial facts are proved, by the spouses themselves.</p>
